# Documentation policy

## Source of truth

- Durable project knowledge: versioned under `docs/`, `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `MODERNIZATION.md`, `AGENTS.md`.
- Shared work state: **GitHub Issues, Projects, Milestones, PRs**.
- Volatile working material: **`.local/`** and **`references/`** (both gitignored).

## Version in Git

Architecture, build/setup, protocols, conventions, security, release procedure,
contribution workflow, stable modernization decisions (`docs/DECISIONS.md`), and
docs required to understand delivered behavior.

## Do not commit

Task lists, session trackers, agent scratchpads, prompt collections, exploratory
notes, unvalidated research, audit drafts, external source trees, or duplicated
GitHub status.

## When to update

Only when a change affects durable documented behavior, setup, architecture,
security, compatibility, or release procedures:

- User-visible: `CHANGELOG.md`
- Protocol: matching `docs/30_protocols/**` page
- Security: `docs/40_quality/security/**` or `.github/SECURITY.md`
- Doc drift: fix or record in `docs/00_index/KNOWN_INCONSISTENCIES.md`

Search for an existing doc before creating a new file. Prefer extending over
duplicating status/roadmap content.

## Doc map (canonical locations)

- `docs/00_index/` — navigation, decisions, known inconsistencies
- `docs/10_dev/` — build, contributing, technical roadmap
- `docs/20_arch/` — architecture
- `docs/30_protocols/<proto>/` — protocol specs and compatibility
- `docs/40_quality/` — security, testing, audits
- `docs/50_user/` — end-user guides

Developer docs: English, Markdown, link claims to code paths or specs.
